WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:07.326
Look at a cat, a wooden chair and a stone.

2
00:00:07.546 --> 00:00:13.351
The cat is living. The chair was
once alive. The stone never was.

3
00:00:13.851 --> 00:00:18.309
Every living thing does four
things. We call them life processes.

4
00:00:18.529 --> 00:00:24.938
Nutrition means taking in food. A cat eats.
A plant makes its own food from sunlight.

5
00:00:25.158 --> 00:00:31.381
Growth means getting bigger. A kitten
grows into a cat, a seedling into a tree.

6
00:00:31.601 --> 00:00:39.031
Movement means moving by itself. A cat runs.
A plant turns its leaves towards the light.

7
00:00:39.251 --> 00:00:46.171
Reproduction means making more of your own
kind. Cats have kittens. Plants make seeds.

8
00:00:46.391 --> 00:00:50.338
A cat does all four. So
does a tree, only slowly.

9
00:00:50.838 --> 00:00:55.482
Some things are not alive now, but
they came from something that was.

10
00:00:55.702 --> 00:00:59.324
A tree is cut down. The
wood is no longer alive.

11
00:00:59.544 --> 00:01:03.399
The wood is made into a chair.
The chair was once alive.

12
00:01:03.619 --> 00:01:08.031
A woollen jumper was a sheep's
coat. A paper book was a tree too.

13
00:01:08.251 --> 00:01:12.523
A leather shoe was once alive
too: leather is animal skin.

14
00:01:13.023 --> 00:01:17.063
A stone, a metal spoon, a
drinking glass, a plastic cup.

15
00:01:17.283 --> 00:01:21.138
None of these ever ate, grew,
moved by itself or had young.

16
00:01:21.358 --> 00:01:25.630
They were never alive. So there
are three groups, not two.

17
00:01:25.850 --> 00:01:30.680
To sort a thing, ask where it came
from. Wood and paper come from trees.

18
00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:36.892
Scientists find things out in five ways.
These are the five types of enquiry.

19
00:01:37.112 --> 00:01:40.734
Research means looking it
up. How far away is the Moon?

20
00:01:40.954 --> 00:01:45.923
In a fair test, you change one thing.
Does more water make a plant grow taller?

21
00:01:46.143 --> 00:01:52.831
Observing over time: watch something
change. How does a tadpole change each week?

22
00:01:53.051 --> 00:01:58.438
Identifying and classifying: sort things
into groups by what they are like.

23
00:01:58.658 --> 00:02:05.577
Pattern seeking: measure lots of things and find
a pattern. Do taller children have bigger feet?

24
00:02:06.077 --> 00:02:11.464
A scientist's question is one you can
answer by looking, testing or looking up.

25
00:02:11.684 --> 00:02:15.353
Do snails move faster on wet
ground? You can test that.

26
00:02:15.573 --> 00:02:20.728
Time the same snail over the same
distance, first on wet ground, then on dry.

27
00:02:20.948 --> 00:02:23.502
Are snails nice? You cannot test that.

28
00:02:23.722 --> 00:02:30.456
So ask: how would I find out? Today,
you will ask a question you can test.

29
00:02:30.956 --> 00:02:36.204
Living things take in food, grow,
move and make more of their own kind.

30
00:02:36.424 --> 00:02:42.414
Wood, paper, wool and leather were once
alive. Rock, metal and glass never were.

31
00:02:42.634 --> 00:02:47.232
Scientists find out in five ways,
and ask questions they can test.
